  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe long term period begins on Sunday night with upper level ridging \nextending from the Deep South towards New England and associated \nsurface high pressure near New England and the eastern Great Lakes. \nThis high pressure regime will keep a vertically stacked low \npressure system well east of Georgia off the mid-Atlantic coast and \npromote dry conditions and PoPs near 0% across the Southeast for the \nmajority of next week. Strong radiational cooling overnight will \nlead to low temperatures in the mid to upper 30s in far north \nGeorgia and low to mid 40s elsewhere across the area on Monday \nmorning. High temperatures on Monday will rise into the upper 60s to \nlow 70s. With plenty of sunshine and increasing 1000-500 mb \nthicknesses, temperatures will be on the rise through midweek. By \nWednesday and through the remainder of the week, lows will be in the \nmid 40s to low 50s while highs will largely be in the mid to upper \n70s.",
